#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560f6e49-18ff-5174-980c-720bcf8ef38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320449} {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} {:answer/id #uuid "957940e7-7f35-58a3-a0e4-daa55621d3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320451} {:answer/id #uuid "68f85aa4-9ff4-5d41-b9a6-047f340b4436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320452} {:answer/id #uuid "d781e2b1-7b73-53f5-aa5e-9598d05ee3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6da3f2-ab9e-5fc4-b2f0-3c180a33303a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "067c622d-d840-5844-b7eb-4a94820f230e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114272" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
